      "headline": "Week 1: Everybody is undefeated and already wrong",
      "writeup": "Five seasons of receipts have brought us here. Andrew enters 2026 as the first two-time champion, Jim and Tommy own the other banners, and everybody else gets the annual privilege of explaining why this is finally their year. Keepers have been locked, Yahoo has handed out grades ranging from A+ to outright disrespect, and our own board disagrees with enough of it to guarantee that somebody is going to be insufferable by Monday night. That is what Week 1 is for. Everybody is undefeated, everybody's roster still makes sense in his own head, and none of the waiver mistakes have happened yet.\n\nThe Moose Knuckles open against Predacious Fungi in a matchup where our model is much less charitable to Max than Max is probably being to himself. Jim came out of the draft with an A roster and the kind of veteran firepower that can make a projection look boring: Derrick Henry and De'Von Achane at running back, Jaxon Smith-Njigba and Ladd McConkey at receiver, George Kittle at tight end, and Jayden Daniels behind the wheel. Max has legitimate skill-position talent — James Cook, Omarion Hampton, Tee Higgins, Garrett Wilson, Emeka Egbuka and Tucker Kraft is not a bad foundation — but the quarterback plan is the giant blinking light on the dashboard. Our model gives Jim a 68.2% edge. If Max solves QB, this gets more interesting. Until then, Moose Knuckles is the pick.\n\nTDs In Your Face against Kareem all over your Hunt is the first game where the rankings and the matchup model start arguing with each other. We graded Billy's draft slightly higher, but the model gives Nick a narrow 55.8% edge. Nick can throw Gibbs, Kyren, Drake London, Waddle and Jameson Williams at you, which is enough weekly explosion to cover a lot of sins. The sin in question is leaving the draft without a tight end. Billy, meanwhile, had no keeper discounts and still opened with Christian McCaffrey and CeeDee Lamb before turning the rest of the draft into a pile of upside bets. This feels like the game most likely to make one preseason opinion look stupid immediately. I'll go against the model and take Billy in a coin flip, mostly because I trust the top-end CMC/CeeDee combination to carry the weird parts of the roster for one week.\n\nOur Game of the Week is Revenge of the period bloods against The Eviscerators, also known as The Trial of Yahoo's Algorithm. Yahoo gave Andrew an A+, projected him to 13-1, and effectively handed him the September Lombardi. Yahoo gave Nate an F and projected him to 1-13, which is the kind of prediction that should come with either a detailed explanation or an apology form. Our board has Andrew second and Nate fifth, so we agree Andrew is better — we just don't agree that these teams belong on different planets. Andrew's Bijan/Breece/Nico core is nasty, and Pickens plus Olave at keeper prices gives him the kind of structural advantage that wins leagues. Nate has no keepers but walked out with Ja'Marr Chase, A.J. Brown, Josh Jacobs, Lamar Jackson and Brian Thomas Jr. That is not a one-win collection of football players. The model still gives Andrew a strong 71.5% edge, and I'm picking The Eviscerators, but this is the first chance for Nate to turn Yahoo's F into a receipt worth framing.\n\nCriterus meets The A Gap in a matchup between our draft winner and one of our biggest Yahoo disagreements. Chris is #1 on our board because there is almost nowhere to poke a clean hole: Jonathan Taylor, Justin Jefferson, Malik Nabers, Bucky Irving, Terry McLaurin, Jalen Hurts, Marvin Harrison Jr., Kyle Pitts and Cam Skattebo at keeper cost is absurd depth for a ten-team league. Adam is not coming empty-handed. Puka Nacua, Ashton Jeanty, Rashee Rice, Drake Maye, David Montgomery, TreVeyon Henderson and Sam LaPorta give The A Gap enough ceiling to wreck any projection if two or three young pieces hit together. The model only makes Chris a 58.3% favorite, which is much closer than the draft rankings suggest. I still take Criterus, but this is exactly the kind of Week 1 game where the supposed favorite spends Sunday afternoon wondering why all that beautiful depth is scoring eight points at a time.\n\nThe Mustache riders and Pump and Go close out the opening slate with another disagreement between the model and our rankings. The model likes TomD at 59.8%; our draft board has Tommy fourth and TomD seventh. TomD's case starts with Amon-Ra and Kenneth Walker, then gets unfair when Brock Bowers shows up as an eighth-round keeper. Tommy answers with Saquon Barkley, Chase Brown, Trey McBride and Josh Allen before we even get to the discounted DeVonta Smith and Tyler Warren. This is probably the cleanest heavyweight matchup of the week. TomD has the positional cheat code at tight end; Tommy has more concentrated nuclear material at the top of the roster. I'm taking Pump and Go in a close one and accepting that the model may have this screenshot ready for me by Monday.\n\nSo the official Week 1 picks are Jim over Max, Billy over Nick, Andrew over Nate, Chris over Adam and Tommy over TomD. Three favorites, two minor rebellions, and five opportunities to look ridiculous before the season is four days old. More importantly, the league is back. The standings are clean, the FAAB accounts are full, every keeper still looks smart, and nobody has had to stare at 28 bench points while losing by three. Enjoy that innocence while it lasts. By next week somebody will be 0-1 with a grievance, somebody will have spent too much money on a waiver claim, and somebody will insist the loss was completely unforeseeable despite all of us having the screenshot.",
